<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Goiás</h1><p class="st-meta">1744 CE – 1821 CE · capital Vila Boa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A gold-mining captaincy in the heart of Brazil, opened by Paulista bandeirantes, whose capital Vila Boa preserves its colonial character.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1722 the bandeirante <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-bueno-da-silva">Bartolomeu Bueno da Silva</a>, son of the explorer known as Anhanguera, led an expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> into the central plateau and found gold along the Vermelho river in 1725. Mining camps grew up, and in 1744 the crown separated the region from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-paulo">Captaincy of São Paulo</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Vila Boa</a>, now Goiás Velho, as its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The captaincy's economy was based on alluvial gold mined by enslaved Africans and taxed by the crown. Settlements were scattered and linked to the coast by long mule trails. Conflicts with indigenous peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayapo">Kayapó</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xavante">Xavante</a> were frequent, and the government founded aldeias to settle them. By the late 18th century gold had run out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching became the mainstay.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goias-velho">Goiás</a> became a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> in 1821. In the 20th century its territory hosted the new national capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a>, and the planned city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goiania">Goiânia</a> replaced Goiás Velho as state capital. The old town became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1744 CE – 1821 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.41%;width:1.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1744 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vila Boa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bartolomeu Bueno da Silva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Goyaz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=captaincy-of-goias" aria-label="Captaincy of Goiás on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3394 2737 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy of Goiás lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3714 2993L3944 2917M3714 2993L3718 3265"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3718 3265h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3714 2993h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3714 2993h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vila Boa. <a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=captaincy-of-goias">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
